{
  "comment": "Conversations continue — inapp-platform-contract.md section 2.6, CONTINUITY.md section D. A chat that passes the memory gate opens with ONE server frame, {\"type\":\"history\",\"turns\":[{id, role: \"user\"|\"assistant\", text, at: ISO-8601 UTC, channel: \"chat\"|\"voice\"}],\"continued\":true}, before any agent message. Three sections, every SDK runs all three. `frames`: the frame as it arrives → the turns it restores (role in the SDKs' words: customer | agent), or null when the frame is ignored (turns not a list). A turn that cannot be read (not an object, a role other than user/assistant, text not a string or blank, `at` missing or not an ISO-8601 time) is skipped; a channel other than \"voice\" is chat; a missing or repeated id becomes \"h<index in the list>\". `restore`: steps on a chat's transcript — {agent} a finished agent turn; {customer, delivery?} the customer's message (sent unless delivery says sending or failed); {opened} the connection opened, so what was waiting went out (sending → sent); {history} the frame arrived — then the turns. The rule: a history that restores at least one turn REPLACES every turn that was settled when the connection opened, and every turn an earlier history restored; it keeps, after the restored turns and in order, the turns still sending or failed and everything said since the connection opened (with no {opened} yet: only the turns still sending or failed). Restored turns are delivered, not live, not volunteered, restored = true, with their own time and channel. An empty or unreadable history changes nothing. In `turns`: a field absent from an expected turn means restored false, channel chat, and (customer turns) delivery sent; `at` and `id`, when given, are compared; live and proactive are false for every restored turn. React Native has no failed delivery: there a failed step is a waiting (pending) message. `rows`: what a chat window draws for the turns, at `now`, on a device whose zone is `timeZone` (a fixed offset of `utcOffsetMinutes`) — {turn: i} a bubble; {voice: [first, last]} ONE \"Voice call · {time of first}\" row for a run of consecutive voice-channel turns on one local day; {day} a separator: \"today\", \"yesterday\", or the local date YYYY-MM-DD (each SDK writes it in its own words: Today, Yesterday, a date). A separator goes before the first row when its day is not today, and wherever the local day changes between rows. `fresh` (DESIGN.md \"Chat window\" → A new conversation under earlier lines, 0.14.0): steps as in `restore`. When the first agent turn after an {opened} arrives with no {history} between them, the server began a new conversation (any {history} frame means it continued, even one that restores nothing: there is then no check): take the CURRENT conversation — the turns settled at that open that come after the last marker (all of them when there is none). None → nothing. No line of the customer's after the last marker — settled, still waiting at that open, or sent since — → the settled ones are removed (an opening nobody answered: the new opening replaces it; an earlier marker stays). Otherwise → a marker before the first turn after the last marker that was not settled at that open. A {history} that restores at least one turn removes every marker before a turn it replaced. Expected: `turns` as in `restore`; `marks` the indices into `turns` of the turns a marker row (\"Disconnected · new conversation\") is drawn before. Written by hand from CONTINUITY.md section D; the React Native SDK (protocol.ts readHistoryFrame / foldChat / chatRows) and every native SDK are tested against it. `display`: what the window draws of the pieces the design can leave out (DESIGN.md \"Chat window\" → Booked calls, Earlier messages, New conversation), one rule for every SDK (the React Native SDK's chatPieces): `chat` is the session's chat tokens as they arrive (absent, or a value that is not a boolean — `upcomingTitle` not a string — is the default: on, and the SDK's words); `turns` the chat's transcript (restored true for an earlier conversation); `upcoming` how many booked calls there are. Expected: `shown` the ids of the turns drawn, in order (chat.showHistory false leaves out every restored turn — the agent still has them); `rows` what those turns draw, in the `rows` notation, indices into `shown`; `starters` whether the brand's starters may show (nothing restored on screen and nothing said by the customer — so a hidden history opens like a new chat); `newConversation` whether the header's compose control shows (chat.newConversation not false, and a restored turn on screen); `card` whether the booked-calls card shows (chat.showUpcomingCalls not false, and at least one call); `cardTitle` its title (chat.upcomingTitle trimmed, else \"Upcoming call\" for one call and \"Upcoming calls\" otherwise). 0.14.0 adds sessions: a history turn may carry \"session\" (the id of the session it was said in: a chat connection's session, or a call's id for voice turns); the SDK keeps it on the turn as `session` (null when absent, empty or not a string) and compares it wherever an expected turn names it. `sessionFrame`: the server's {\"type\":\"session\",\"id\",\"at\"} frame, sent once when a chat connection starts, gives the chat's sessionId (null for an id that is not a non-empty string); a live turn carries the sessionId of the connection that carries it: a line typed before the chat connected, or said before the session frame arrived, takes that connection's session once the frame arrives, never a stray session with no id; an identity change clears sessionId, and the unsent lines it keeps join the next session. `sessions`: the chat's sessions, derived from its turns oldest first — consecutive turns with the same session value (null included) make one entry {id, startedAt (first turn's at), endedAt (last turn's at), channel (\"voice\" when every turn is voice, else \"chat\"), turns (count), current (id is not null and equals sessionId)}.",
